;; A `(fn ...)` type cannot carry refinements, and says so.
;;
;; Before this, the annotation was ACCEPTED by the reader and never peeled, so
;; the contract type survived into the signature and every use of the value
;; failed with `expected { _ : ? | ... }, got int` -- naming a type the
;; programmer never wrote, at a line they did not annotate.
;;
;; This is the fourth site to hit the unpeeled-contract defect; CT0's note on
;; `rt_peel_contract` lists the first three (defn return types, fn parameters,
;; typeclass instance-method parameters). It is not a missing peel, though:
;; there is nowhere for the predicate to ride, because a function type has no
;; slot for it. So it is REJECTED rather than peeled-and-discarded -- silently
;; dropping the predicate would be worse, since the annotation would then read
;; like a guarantee that is being checked and is not.
;;
;; Rejected with the gate OFF too: the `#refine{...}` syntax is the contract
;; layer, which is always on.
;;
;; The underlying limit is higher-order checking. A function value with refined
;; parameters may be passed and called freely -- its own entry checks still run,
;; so nothing unsound follows -- but the refinement is invisible through the
;; function type, so neither the body that calls it nor the caller that supplies
;; it is checked statically.
(defn safe-div [a : int b : int] : int (/ a b))

(defn apply-param [f : (fn [int #refine{ v : int | (not= v 0) }] int) x : int] : int
  (f 10 x))

(defn apply-result [f : (fn [int int] #refine{ r : int | (> r 0) })] : int
  (f 1 2))

(defn main [] : int (println (apply-param safe-div 2)) 0)
